WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.670
Today, we'll be speaking with Josh Dubin,
who is a defense attorney and ambassador,

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:09.400
advisor for the Innocence Project, a criminal
justice reform advocate and also host of the

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.680
podcast Wrongful Conviction Junk Science.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.850
Josh, it's so great to have you on today.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.240
Thanks for having me.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:17.630
Happy to be here.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:24.909
So just to start with, can you talk a little
bit about how you ended up working in the

00:00:24.909 --> 00:00:30.519
area of wrongful conviction and sort of the
path that led you to that?

00:00:30.519 --> 00:00:31.519
Sure.

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:44.040
You know, I've always been interested in criminal
justice reform, and I got a call when I was

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:52.710
like in my late 20s, I had this odd path because
I was known as a jury, as a jury expert.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:58.310
So I would get hired by criminal defense lawyers
to help them pick juries.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:00:59.750
And that was the way it started.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:02.390
In some civil attorneys as well.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:10.229
So I got a call from Barry Scheck, who I had
known from watching the O.J. Simpson trial

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:12.460
when I was in college.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:19.049
I actually thought it was my brother pranking
me so I'd never return the call.

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:26.719
And then the person that the person that referred
me called me names, Gerry Lefcourt, who was

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:34.350
a civil rights icon who represented the Panther
21, the Black Black Panthers at that that

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.850
their famous trial.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.749
And Abbie Hoffman.

00:01:37.749 --> 00:01:40.409
And he called me and said, what's your problem?

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:43.220
I referred you to Barry Scheck and you don't
call him back.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:52.710
So I called them back and I met Barry and
helped him with a case and it sort of took

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:53.710
off from there.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:59.759
Shortly thereafter, Barry Scheck and Peter
Neufeld had a civil practice, civil rights

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:06.350
practice, where they were beginning to represent
people that they had helped exonerate through

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:10.170
at the Innocence Project in their civil suits.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:16.340
And I began working with him and we really
hit it off and it sort of took off from there.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.340
So big, big picture.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:25.190
You can find all sorts of different reports
that that claim one in 25 people on death

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:28.170
row is innocent or some percentages.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:33.190
There's all sorts of different numbers that
that are applied, generally speaking, to folks

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:36.310
who have been convicted and are in prison.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:42.380
What is a good way for the lay person to wrap
their mind around what portion of people convicted

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:50.800
for serious crimes are are are actually innocent
of those crimes?

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:56.600
You know, it's difficult to tell because you
don't know how many people are out there until

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.430
you take on their cases.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:02.810
So I don't put much stock into the statistics,
but I'll give you one that I do put a lot

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.670
of stock into.

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:07.650
And this is only dealing with one state.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:15.070
You know, the state of Florida has 29 exonerations
off of death row.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.060
Twenty nine.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.900
They have only executed ninety nine people
in the state of Florida.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.730
So I'm a shitty math student.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:29.700
But if you do the quick math that shows you
that a staggering percentage of people that

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:35.670
were sitting on death row awaiting execution
were actually innocent.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:41.610
I mean, I was the lead attorney for chlamydia,
Gary, who was the twenty ninth exonerated

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:42.610
from death row.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:49.070
And I'm currently representing James Daley,
who I think should be the 30th exonerated.

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:55.700
He's either going to be the 30th exonerated
or the one hundredth person executed.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.450
So I hope it's the former and not the latter.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:03.920
And I'm working real hard with my team to
make sure that that isn't the case or that,

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.090
you know, that is the case, that he gets exonerated.

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:13.670
But look, it's hard to know what percentage
I would venture to say that it's much higher

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.480
than people think.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:23.600
And it's a scary number because even one innocent
person sitting on death row rows too much.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.260
So in interviews and documentaries I've seen
about the Innocence Project and Barry and

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:34.600
Peter, one of the things that seems very important
in so to set the context.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:39.710
The Innocence Project gets far more requests
than the number that they can take in terms

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:41.690
of the number of cases that can be handled.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.570
Many of these cases take years to be resolved.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:48.010
So there's a there's a selection process that
happens there.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:52.180
And certainly it's important how strongly
correct me if I'm wrong.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:57.610
You feel that the individual in question is
innocent, but how much of the calculus, again,

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:02.000
given that there are limited resources and
a limited number of cases you can take?

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:11.500
How much of the calculus is about the likelihood
that you could actually get a conviction overturned?

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:17.150
That's a good part of the calculus, you know,
and different criminal justice reform organizations

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:22.990
have different criteria for how they go about
assessing cases and whether or not they'll

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:24.180
take them.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:28.970
You know, I have a little bit more liberty
because I don't work at the Innocence Project,

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.600
although I'm an ambassador for the Innocence
Project.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:38.100
So sometimes I take on Innocence Project cases
and that they're they're my first love, if

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.720
you will.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:45.830
But, you know, I can say that different people
do this work for different reasons.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:48.919
Some people take a look at a case and say,
you know what?

00:05:48.919 --> 00:05:56.120
There are terrible legal violations here,
constitutional violations, and that if you

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:00.400
know there's something worth fighting for
here, regardless of their assessment of a

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:03.770
person's guilt or innocence.

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:06.990
And for me, that's important.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:14.930
And it's also important to me, you know, I
think that, look, we're human beings.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:20.880
I think that the more I feel that someone
is innocent, the harder I think I'll fight,

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.930
whether on a conscious or subconscious level.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:28.450
So I think that.

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:31.810
Likelihood of success is definitely a factor.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:39.710
But I can tell you that all of these exonerations
are a an arduous battle, a steep uphill climb.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:42.980
You're fighting against very tall odds.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:47.940
So I hope that that was a long winded way
of answering your question.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:48.940
Yeah.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:57.770
I mean, how much does a gut instinct factor
into your belief about a possible case?

00:06:57.770 --> 00:06:59.820
A possible person?

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:02.620
You'll represent a client's guilt or innocence.

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:03.650
How much of it is gut?

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:07.220
And how much of it is reviewing the facts?

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:16.850
I try not to let my God lead me in one direction
or another, and I try to to base it on on

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:19.389
facts or or absence of facts.

00:07:19.389 --> 00:07:25.900
And there's a very there's a very particular
reason why I sort of ascribe to that.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.470
And why what is that?

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:32.690
Because it's got it's the gut of detectives
and law enforcement.

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:42.080
Some as some oftentimes well-intentioned but
extraordinarily misguided, that leads to the

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:45.889
wrongful accusation in the first place.

00:07:45.889 --> 00:07:47.259
Which leads to the cause.

00:07:47.259 --> 00:07:55.060
Confession was relates to making up evidence
to make the the ends justify the means that,

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:59.890
you know, it's a it's a very dangerous path
to go down.

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:05.540
Because I'm often humbled by evidence that
or the lack of evidence that conflicts with

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:06.540
my gut.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.310
So I try not to let my gut get too involved.

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:15.770
And because, again, I think that, you know,
it's oftentimes detectives feel like in their

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:21.490
gut that if a spouse is murdered, it's the
surviving spouse that did it.

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:25.500
And they started focusing on them because
that's their gut.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:31.430
And it can lead to tunnel vision and confirmation
bias and a whole host of problems that are

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:37.130
a pretty terrifying slippery slope for the
accused.

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:42.930
There are a very long list of of reasons and
different ways in which innocent people can

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:43.940
end up convicted.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:49.760
And as you're talking about, tunnel vision
from investigators can be a way where where

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:55.800
somebody becomes the suspect relatively early
on at the exclusion of maybe others.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:00.290
Junk science is another aspect of this.

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:05.940
And for example, forensic dentistry is one
that's been explored significantly and has

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:10.830
been involved in in a lot of retrials or exonerations.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:16.990
Can you talk a little bit about how would
you sort of categorize the different buckets?

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:20.220
That would be the main ways in which innocent
people end up convicted.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:22.529
Yeah, no, that's a great question.

00:09:22.529 --> 00:09:27.800
You know, one of the things it's also a natural
Segway for me to plug the podcast, but, you

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:32.980
know, on our podcast, Wrongful Conviction
Junk Science, we explore a different discipline

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:35.320
of junk science on every episode.

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:38.839
And it's not something that I'm plucking out
of thin air.

00:09:38.839 --> 00:09:40.010
I am doing it.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:48.279
Law, in large part based on the National Academy
of Sciences report in 2009 that examined these

00:09:48.279 --> 00:09:55.029
various disciplines of forensic dentistry
and essentially found that the only one that

00:09:55.029 --> 00:10:01.890
that is reputable and has any bankable, reliable
science behind that is DNA.

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:07.910
So we examined everything from bite marks,
as you mentioned, forensic dentistry to arson

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:13.520
to, you know.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:19.700
Footwear impressions to ballistics, to gunshot
residue, to to fingerprints.

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:20.850
Think about that.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:26.450
We have always been taught in pop culture
on true crime shows that fingerprints are

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:30.190
the gold standard and they're not.

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:36.730
And if you really examine any of these disciplines
of forensic science, you find out that even

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:42.410
attaching the word forensic to it or calling
it a science is a misnomer because many of

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:51.550
them were born in in basements and at the
hands of some crackpot that did not get even

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:57.830
minimal buy in from the scientific community,
much less apply the scientific method.

00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:00.210
And they get accepted in one case.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:05.700
And because of art, our system of legal precedent,
they all order all of a sudden start spreading

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.580
through the justice system.

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:16.430
And, you know, we get these, you know, various
forms of of alleged science that you need

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:34.899
a 40 hour correspondence course to get declared
an expert and then, you know, you end up with

00:11:34.899 --> 00:11:50.600
the real problem.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:12:14.560
My full conversation with Josh Dubin will
be available on The David Pakman Show YouTube

00:12:14.560 --> 00:13:08.290
channel very, very soon at YouTube dot com
slash the David Pakman show.

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:48.339
We will go to 

00:13:48.339 --> 00:16:53.079
a quick break and be 

00:16:53.079 --> 00:17:29.960
back 

00:17:29.960 --> 00:20:11.700
after this.

